Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/gFoz_B_35Xc/m/gweKeuvdAgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi all，
I read  in chapter 8 of  Bayesian inference with INLA  that INLA can be applied to state space，I wonder whether my model is supported by INLA , my model is as follows:

Q_{t}=exp(y_{t})+exp(y_{t-1})+exp(y_{t-2})
y_{t}=tau_{t}+c_{t}
tau_{t}=tau_{t-1}+d_{t-1}+eta_{t}
d_{t}=d_{t-1}+V_{t}
c_{t}=a_{1}c_{t-1}+a_{2}c_{t-2}+epsilon_{t}

where eta_t, V_t and epsilon_t follow iid ,the parameter are parameter is the corresponding variance and a_1,a_2

Many thanks!

From u399bf2e734c020dc at groups.google.com  Thu Mar 10 10:47:35 2022
From: u399bf2e734c020dc at groups.google.com (Sylvan Benaksas)
Date: Thu, 10 Mar 2022 10:47:35 +0000
Subject: How does inla.mesh.1d bin time between knots
Message-ID: <oT5BMfNwY8Y.4AGcoAvvAgAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/oT5BMfNwY8Y/m/4AGcoAvvAgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi everyone,

I have 21 years of data from 2000 - 2020 that I want to divide into 7 groups of three

I have code

gtime <- as.integer(seq(2000, 2020, length = 7))

> gtime
[1] 2000 2003 2006 2010 2013 2016 2020

mesh.t <- inla.mesh.1d(loc=gtime, degree = 1)

will the first group be 2000, 2001 and 2002 etc. ?

Many thanks,

Sylvan

From uafb003e064114795 at groups.google.com  Thu Mar 10 13:55:55 2022
From: uafb003e064114795 at groups.google.com (Finn Lindgren)
Date: Thu, 10 Mar 2022 13:55:55 +0000
Subject: Re: [r-inla] How does inla.mesh.1d bin time between knots
Message-ID: <oT5BMfNwY8Y.o0AixVL5AgAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <oT5BMfNwY8Y.4AGcoAvvAgAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
References: <oT5BMfNwY8Y.4AGcoAvvAgAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/oT5BMfNwY8Y/m/o0AixVL5AgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

By default, inla.mesh.1d defines a piecewise linear basis system, so it does not "group" the time points. Instead, it defines a piecewise linear function with nodes at the input "loc" values (or "knots").
So in your example, 2000 will be mapped to the first node, 2001 will be mapped to 2/3 times the first node value plus 1/3 times the second node value, etc.

Finn

