Hi Finn!

Thanks, very clear now. I made some tests with rw2 and it's working well. I was not aware of this association between rw2 and cubic splines.

Silas

Em quarta-feira, 30 de março de 2022 às 15:09:12 UTC-3, finn.l...@gmail.com escreveu:
> In the bayesian setting with Gaussian priors on the coefficients, splines are just special cases of Gaussian processes. In particular penalized splines; when you have enough basis functions/knots, a proper penalty yields a well defined continuous time/space Gaussian process.
>
> Finn
>
> > On 30 Mar 2022, at 19:06, Finn Lindgren <finn.l...@gmail.com> wrote:
> >
> > ﻿The rw2 model is associated with regular cubic splines, via the int (f’k(t))^2 dt penalty.
> > You can also use 1d Matern proper covariance models, via 1d mesh models and inla.spde2.pcmatern.
> > The extrapolation behaviour of rw1 models is inherent in that model choice, so if you need other extrapolation behaviour you need to change your model.
> >
> > Finn
> >
> > > On 30 Mar 2022, at 19:00, 'Silas C Principe' via R-inla discussion group <r-inla-disc...@googlegroups.com> wrote:
> > >
> > > ﻿Hi Finn! Many thanks for the explanation. It's much clear for me now.
> > >
> > > I will try to look for other ways to include a non-linear effect, maybe splines. Although the rw2 may also work. This extrapolation behavior can be quite problematic in some cases (specially for forecasting), so let's see.
> > >
> > > Silas
